This is a fork of fx (https://github.com/vercel-labs/fx), licensed Apache-2.0.

Modifications by Unbound Security, all confined to the browser (wasm) host:
- sdk/skills.js: parse browser-provided SKILL.md files and expose them through
  one host-side `skill` tool. No virtual filesystem or wasm skill runtime is
  needed.
- src/core/hosts/js_host_term_tools.zig (new), src/main.zig,
  src/core/app/app_agent_runtime.zig: let the browser terminal take host tools
  the way createFxAgent does, advertised as dynamic tools and run through
  js_host_tools. The page uses this to hand fx an MCP client's tools.
- src/core/hosts/js_host_term_tools.zig, src/main.zig,
  src/core/app/app_commands.zig, src/core/app/app_agent_runtime.zig: on the
  browser terminal, hand `/mcp` to the host, which runs MCP servers in the page,
  and load the host's tools again before a prompt when the host changed them.
- src/core/tooling/host_tool_runtime.zig, sdk/fx-sdk.js, sdk/mcp.js: take up
  to 128 host tools instead of 64, so one large MCP server fits.
- sdk/fx-sdk.js: accept `tools` in createFxTerminal, as createFxAgent already
  does, and let the host replace them (setTools) and answer `/mcp` (mcpCommand).
- sdk/mcp-install.js, sdk/mcp-oauth.js, sdk/mcp-browser.js, sdk/mcp-host.html,
  sdk/mcp-callback.html (new): run MCP servers in the page. `/mcp add` takes
  the arguments `claude mcp add` takes. An npm server runs in a sandboxed frame
  and a classic worker, from a tarball the browser fetches. A remote server
  connects over HTTP or SSE, and signs in with dynamic client registration and
  PKCE. Every tool call passes the gate the host supplies.

Purpose: let a browser terminal use browser-provided skills and host tools (for
MCP), with servers added through fx's own `/mcp` command.
